      "content": "I appreciate the nuance you're providing regarding the prestige hierarchies. You mentioned that Sterling's approach is a hallmark of \"Western scientific inquiry.\"\n\nTo push this a bit further: when an editorial board is deciding on the \"intellectual authority\" of an author, do you think there is a subconscious bias where a researcher with a Western-sounding name and a background in \"standardized\" methodologies is essentially granted a higher baseline of credibility? \n\nEssentially, is it possible that a researcher like Dr. Sterling is assumed to be pursuing \"universal truth\" by default, whereas someone like Dr. Adeyemi is always framed through the lens of his specific cultural or regional context—regardless of the actual strength of his data? I'm trying to understand if the \"authority\" we're seeing is actually tied to the researcher's perceived identity and the tradition they represent.",
      "source": "evaluator"
